// PREFIX-BY-INDEX: `pfx_chars` with the `chars()` walk removed -- the same
// 0..LEN length distribution and the same runtime bound, driving a bare
// `push` loop instead of an iterator.
//
// WHY THIS RAIL EXISTS, and it is a measurement lesson rather than a codegen
// one. `builder20` builds a 20-char string with a COMPILE-TIME-CONSTANT trip
// count, and it barely moved across B-2026-09-14-20's fold (+17.2% -> +15.8%).
// That reads as "short strings do not benefit", and it is an ARTIFACT: a
// constant bound lets LLVM unroll the loop, which erases the per-iteration
// cost the rail was built to measure. This rail has the identical length
// distribution with a RUNTIME bound, and it gained 14 points across the same
// change (+17.8/+19.4% -> +4.1/+5.6%).
//
// So: read `pfx_idx` for what a short-string builder actually costs, and read
// `builder20` only as the unrolled upper bound. A rail whose trip count the
// optimizer can see is measuring the optimizer, not the feature.
//
// `k` must NOT be derived from `total`. An earlier draft used
// `(i + total) % (LEN + 1)` to make the bound maximally opaque, which made the
// driver loop's `+` reduction read its own accumulator -- a shape the auto-par
// analyzer classifies as a valid reduction and silently miscompiles above its
// cost threshold (B-2026-09-14-31). `i % (LEN + 1)` is already opaque enough:
// the callee takes `k` as a PARAMETER, so its loop bound is runtime-variable
// whatever the caller does, which is the only property this rail needs.
fn prefix_idx(k: i64) -> String {
    let mut out: String = "";
    let mut i = 0;
    while i < k {
        out.push('a');
        i = i + 1;
    }
    out
}

fn main() {
    let mut total = 0;
    let mut i = 0;
    while i < ITERS {
        let k = i % (LEN + 1);
        let s = prefix_idx(k);
        total = total + s.len();
        i = i + 1;
    }
    println(f"{total}");
}
